[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The scene is composed with two halved whole-wheat pita pockets centered on a rustic, matte grey ceramic plate, leaving generous negative space around the edges. The camera is positioned at a 35-degree 3/4 overhead angle, ensuring every component is clearly identifiable. The depth of field is shallow, keeping the near pita sharp with a soft, creamy bokeh on the background pita. Lighting is soft, natural window light from the left, creating gentle shadows and a slight, appetizing sheen on the yogurt-based salad mixture. The setting is a worn, warm oak kitchen table with natural wood grain. Props include a small, partially used lemon and a scattering of fresh green dill leaves on the table surface, along with a blurred wooden spoon resting on the side. The dish consists of two halved pita pockets standing upright or slightly tilted, stuffed generously with a hearty, textured mixture of smashed chickpeas and crumbled extra-firm tofu, held together by a creamy Greek yogurt dressing. Small, diced bits of bright red onion, vibrant green celery, and chopped fresh dill are clearly visible throughout the chunky, rustic filling, which peeks out from beneath a layer of fresh, crisp mixed salad greens lining the pitas. The mixture is coarse and perfectly imperfect. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking and plating — lived-in, warm, authentic. Not a sterile studio setup. Slight natural imperfections: an asymmetric garnish placement, a tiny stray piece of onion on the plate rim, slightly uneven filling edges. Include subtle traces of the cooking process: a small herb leaf on the table, a slight smudge of yogurt near the plate. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature — cozy and inviting, not cold or clinical. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. The image should be indistinguishable from a photograph taken by a professional food photographer for a printed cookbook."
]
